<h2>The setting</h2>
<p>Axis Finance Limited had lent against a mortgage of immovable property situated in Kolkata. When the account soured, the lender moved along the enforcement track that Parliament built for secured creditors: a demand notice dated 23 January 2026 under Section 13(2) of the SARFAESI Act, followed by symbolic possession of the secured assets on 9 April 2026 under Section 13(4). With arbitration contemplated under the loan documents, the lender also petitioned the Delhi High Court under Section 9 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 for interim protection — adding a judicial restraint on top of its contractual and statutory security.</p>

<h2>The reasoning: necessity, not comfort</h2>
<p>Justice Om Prakash Shukla dismissed the petition on a principle that will resonate across lending disputes. Section 9 relief is a response to demonstrated necessity, and the Court found none. Two strands run through the order:</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h4>Apprehension is not enough</h4><p>A "mere apprehension that the Respondents may alienate their assets" cannot justify additional interim relief. The petitioner needed supporting material showing an immediate necessity — and had none.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h4>No duplicative security</h4><p>With the dues already secured by a mortgage and SARFAESI proceedings pending, the restraints sought "would amount to duplicative interim security" — a doubling-up the Court declined to order.</p></div>
</div>
<p>The Court drew support from a recent Division Bench ruling in the lender’s own earlier litigation — <em>Axis Finance Ltd. v. Rishab Mago</em>, FAO(OS)(COMM) 157/2026 — which upheld the refusal of Section 9 relief where the dues were secured by mortgage and SARFAESI enforcement was pending before the Debts Recovery Tribunal. The single-Judge order thus consolidates what is now a consistent Delhi position on the point.</p>

<h2>The doctrinal frame</h2>
<p>Section 9 petitions are decided on the familiar trinity — prima facie case, balance of convenience, irreparable harm — tempered by the principles governing attachment before judgment. A claimant seeking to freeze or secure assets must show that the respondent is acting, or about to act, in a manner designed to defeat the eventual award. For an unsecured claimant, dissipation of the only recoverable assets can be exactly such a showing. A secured creditor stands differently: the mortgage is the protection, and the SARFAESI Act gives it an enforcement engine that needs no interim order. Asking the court for more, without showing that the security itself is threatened or inadequate, inverts the logic of interim relief — it seeks comfort, not protection.</p>

<h2>What lenders and borrowers should take from the order</h2>
<p>For <strong>lenders</strong>, the order is a drafting and strategy note. A Section 9 petition filed alongside SARFAESI measures must plead with specificity: valuation evidence showing the security shortfall, documented attempts to alienate unencumbered assets, diversion of receivables, or conduct defeating the enforcement already underway. Boilerplate apprehensions will fail, and a failed Section 9 petition hands the borrower a judicial finding that the lender is adequately secured — a finding with afterlife in the DRT and in settlement talks. For <strong>borrowers and guarantors</strong>, the order confirms a shield worth raising at the threshold of every lender-side Section 9: where the exposure is mortgage-backed and enforcement is live, the petition should be met with the duplication objection before any contest on the merits of the debt.</p>
<p>The decision also keeps the channels doctrinally clean. The arbitral tribunal remains the forum for the money claim, with Section 17 available for genuinely necessary interim measures once constituted; the SARFAESI process remains the secured creditor’s self-help track, policed by the DRT under Section 17 of that Act. The writ of Section 9 runs between them only where a real, immediate and unprotected risk is shown.</p>
<div class="note">
<p>Before filing a Section 9 petition in a secured lending matter, the first document to prepare is not the petition — it is a candid internal note on what the existing security actually covers. If the honest answer is "the exposure", the petition is likely to do more harm than good.</p>
</div>
